id: rule-whilec-count
title: Counting to 10
topic: Loop
example-of: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
derived: true

task:
  Use the loop rule with consequence to derive this triple. Choose the
  invariant above the steps: the proof succeeds with the first one and
  fails with the second one.

given:
  Loop condition B: $x < 10$

pre: x = 0
post: x = 10

scheme: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
  Initialisation: «P» ==> «I»
  Preservation: {«I» && «x < 10 : B»} x := x + 1 {«I»}
  Conclusion: «I» && «!(x < 10) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
  ---
  {«P»} while x < 10 do x := x + 1 {«Q»}

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while x < 10 do {
    {I && x < 10}
    {R1}
    x := x + 1     // b
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(x < 10)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

choice: I = 0 ≤ x ≤ 10
outcome: proves

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The variable $x$ goes from 0 to 10, one by one, and the loop stops at
  10. So $0 <= x <= 10$ holds each time the condition is evaluated.
set: I = 0 <= x <= 10

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, I
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
  $P$ says $x = 0$, so we replace $x$ by 0 in $I$.
assume: x = 0
calc:
  0 <= x <= 10
  = 0 <= 0 <= 10       // x = 0, by P
  = true               // 0 <= 0 and 0 <= 10
vc: x = 0 ==> 0 <= x <= 10

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the body for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN
focus: b
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «x < 10 : B»} x := x + 1 {«I»}
  We substitute the variable $x$ with $x + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, I)
  = (0 <= x <= 10)[x <- x + 1]     // I
  = 0 <= x + 1 <= 10               // x replaced by x + 1
set: R1 = 0 <= x + 1 <= 10

step: I and B imply R1
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && x < 10, R1
text:
  >>> «I» && «x < 10 : B» ==> «R1»
  We prove the two bounds of $R1$ separately.
  - Lower bound: from $0 <= x$, in $I$, we get $0 <= x + 1$.
  - Upper bound: from $x < 10$, which is $B$, we get $x + 1 <= 10$, since
    $x$ is an integer.
vc: 0 <= x <= 10 && x < 10 ==> 0 <= x + 1 <= 10

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && !(x < 10), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(x < 10) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
  We simplify the left side.
calc:
  0 <= x <= 10 && !(x < 10)
  = 0 <= x <= 10 && x >= 10     // !(x < 10) is x >= 10
  = x = 10                      // x <= 10 and x >= 10
vc: 0 <= x <= 10 && !(x < 10) ==> x = 10
why:
  The left side is $x = 10$, which is $Q$. All three premises hold.

choice: I = 0 ≤ x
outcome: fails

step: The candidate invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  We try the weaker candidate $0 <= x$, without the upper bound.
set: I = 0 <= x

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, I
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
  $P$ says $x = 0$, so we replace $x$ by 0 in $I$.
assume: x = 0
calc:
  0 <= x
  = 0 <= 0       // x = 0, by P
  = true         // 0 <= 0
vc: x = 0 ==> 0 <= x

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the body for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN
focus: b
text:
  >>> {«I» && «x < 10 : B»} x := x + 1 {«I»}
  We substitute the variable $x$ with $x + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, I)
  = (0 <= x)[x <- x + 1]     // I
  = 0 <= x + 1               // x replaced by x + 1
set: R1 = 0 <= x + 1

step: I and B imply R1
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && x < 10, R1
text:
  >>> «I» && «x < 10 : B» ==> «R1»
vc: 0 <= x && x < 10 ==> 0 <= x + 1
why:
  From $0 <= x$ we get $0 <= x + 1$.

step: I and not B do not imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && !(x < 10), Q
text:
  >>> «I» && «!(x < 10) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
  We simplify the left side.
calc:
  0 <= x && !(x < 10)
  = 0 <= x && x >= 10     // !(x < 10) is x >= 10
  = x >= 10               // x >= 10 gives 0 <= x
vc: 0 <= x && !(x < 10) ==> x = 10
status: fails
counterexample: x = 11
why:
  The state $x = 11$ satisfies $x >= 10$ and not $x = 10$. The candidate
  is an invariant, but it is too weak: the bound $x <= 10$ is missing.
